Fix $T\in(0,oo)$ and suppose that $f\in C_0^oo(\R_+\times\R^d)$.
It is well-known that the function
#eq (eq:duhamel) {
    u(t,x) = \int_0^t\int_{\R^d} p(t-s,x-y)f(s,y)dyds,
}
satisfies $u_t = \Delta u + f$ with zero initial condition,
where $p(t,x) = \I_{t>0}(4\pi t)^{-d/2}\exp(-|x|^2/(4t))$, a heat kernel.
Since $||p(t,\cdot)||_1 = 1$ for every $t > 0$, we have
$$
    ||u(t,\cdot)||_p
    <= \int_0^t ||p(t-s,\cdot)*_xf(s,\cdot)||_p ds
    <= \int_0^t ||p(t-s,\cdot)||_1 ||f(s,\cdot)||_p ds
    <= \int_0^t ||f(s,\cdot)||_p ds.
$$
Notice that as $f$ is infinitely differentiable in $x$, same argument shows that
$u\in W_p^{1,2}(T)$. Hence, by the uniqueness on $W_p^{1,2}(T)$,
every function satisfying $u_t = \Delta u + f$ can be represented by
\cref{eq:duhamel} for $t\in[0,T]$ and $x\in\R^d$.

We are going to estimate $u$ with the similar method as in \Cref{sec:lemma:4.8}.
Fix constants $\mu>0$, $\nu>0$, and $\epsilon>0$ will be specified later.
By Young's inequality, we obtain
$$
    \mu|x| <= {\epsilon |x|^2 // 2t} + {\mu^2 t // 2\epsilon},\quad
    e^{\mu|x|}p(t,x) <= \I_{t>0}(4\pi t)^{-d/2}e^{\mu^2t/(2\epsilon)}
    \exp?({(2\epsilon - 1) |x|^2 // 4t})?.
$$
In order that the integral of the right hand side under $x$ is finite,
$\epsilon$ should be in range $(0,1/2)$, and in that case, we have
$$
    \int_{\R^d} e^{\mu|x|}p(t,x) dx <= N(\epsilon,d) e^{\mu^2t/(2\epsilon)}.
$$
Therefore,
useenv align* {
    e^{\mu|x| - \nu t}|u(t,x)|
    &<= e^{\mu|x| - \nu t}\int_0^t\int_{\R^d} p(t-s,x-y) |f(s,y)|dyds \\
    &<= \int_0^t\int_{\R^d} ?| e^{\mu|x-y|-\nu(t-s)} p(t-s,x-y) |? 
    ?| e^{\mu|y| - \nu s}f(s,y) |? dyds \\
    &<= ?( \sup_{s,y} e^{\mu|y| - \nu s} |f(s,y)|)?
    \int_0^t\int_{\R^d} e^{\mu|y|-\nu s} p(s,y) dyds \\
    &<= N \int_0^oo \exp?(-\nu s + {\mu^2 s // 2\epsilon})? ds.
}
Thus if $\mu^2 < 2\nu\epsilon$ and $0 < \epsilon < 1/2$, the integral is finite
and the estimate is meaningful.
Take $\mu=\sqrt{2}$, $\nu=8$, and $\epsilon = 1/4$, for instance.
Then we have the following inequality
$$
    |u(t,x)| <= N e^{-\sqrt{2}|x| + 8t},\quad t\in [0,T],\ x\in\R^d,
$$
where $N = N(d,f)$.

In addition, since $f$ is smooth in $x$, we have $D_x^\alpha u = p * D_x^\alpha
f$ for every multiindex $\alpha$. Therefore, we also obtain the following
inequality:
$$
    |(D_x^\alpha u)(t,x)| <= N e^{-\sqrt{2}|x| + 8t},\quad t\in [0,T],\ x\in\R^d,
$$
where $N = N(d,\alpha,f)$.

